================================================================================
 /etc/sudoers & PRIVILEGE MANAGEMENT GUIDE
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW & PURPOSE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The /etc/sudoers file is a critical security configuration file in Linux. It
controls privilege escalation by defining which users and groups are allowed to
execute commands with elevated (root) privileges.

Primary Locations:
  - Main configuration file:          /etc/sudoers
  - Drop-in configuration directory:  /etc/sudoers.d/

Key Capabilities Controlled by sudoers:
  - Permitted users and groups
  - Target executable commands (must use full binary paths)
  - Target execution contexts (RunAs user and RunAs group)
  - Applicable host systems
  - Password authentication requirements and session timeouts
  - Audit logging behavior
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. SAFE EDITING AND VALIDATION PROCEDURES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WARNING: NEVER edit /etc/sudoers using standard text editors (vi, vim, nano).
Unvalidated edits can corrupt syntax and completely lock administrative access.

Recommended Tool: visudo
The visudo utility locks the configuration file during editing and validates
syntax prior to applying changes.

Common Commands:
  # visudo                           Edit the primary /etc/sudoers file
  # visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/<file>  Create/edit a drop-in configuration file
  # visudo -c                        Validate syntax without opening editor

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. SUDOERS SYNTAX AND ALIAS STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Standard Rule Format:
  user_or_group   host=(run_as_user:run_as_group)   [options:] command

Example Rule:
  harshal ALL=(ALL) ALL

Syntax Breakdown:
  - harshal : User account to which the rule applies.
  -  ALL     : Rule is valid on all host systems.
  -  (ALL)   : Permits command execution as any user/group context.
  -  ALL     : Grants access to execute all commands.

Aliases:
  Aliases simplify management across large environments by grouping entities:

  User_Alias  LINUXADMINS = harshal, admin1, admin2
  Host_Alias  WEBSERVERS  = web01, web02, web03
  Cmnd_Alias  WEB_CMDS    = /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd, \
                            /usr/bin/systemctl status httpd

  # Applied Directive:
  LINUXADMINS WEBSERVERS = (root) WEB_CMDS

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. GROUP DELEGATION & WHEEL GROUP ACCESS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
On RHEL and AlmaLinux systems, administrative sudo access is typically delegated
to members of the "wheel" group.

Group Syntax:
  The '%' character indicates a group name.
  %wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL

Adding Users to the Wheel Group:
  1. Assign user to group:
     # usermod -aG wheel harshal

  2. Verify membership:
     # id harshal
     Output: uid=1001(harshal) gid=1001(harshal) groups=1001(harshal),10(wheel)

  3. Verification:
     The target user must start a new login session for group changes to apply.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. SECURITY PRINCIPLE: LEAST PRIVILEGE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Avoid assigning unrestricted access (ALL=(ALL) ALL) to regular operational
users. Instead, grant explicit binary paths.

Examples:
  1. Single Command Access:
     harshal ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd

  2. Multiple Explicit Commands:
     harshal ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd, \
                        /usr/bin/systemctl status httpd

  3. Unattended / Scripted Execution (NOPASSWD):
     harshal ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd

Security Note on Exclusions (!):
  Do NOT grant ALL commands and attempt to deny specific ones using '!'
  (e.g., ALL, !/bin/bash). Users can easily bypass negative rules via shell
  escapes, text editors, or subshells. Always explicitly ALLOW required binaries.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. MODULAR CONFIGURATION VIA /etc/sudoers.d/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Red Hat recommends using the /etc/sudoers.d/ directory for custom rules
rather than modifying /etc/sudoers directly.

Advantages:
  - Prevents accidental corruption of the main configuration file.
  - Simplifies configuration management and automation (Ansible, Puppet).
  - Enables modular granting and revocation of permissions per application/role.

Standard Workflow:
  1. Create file via visudo:
     # visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/webadmin

  2. Add directive:
     harshal ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart httpd

  3. Validate configuration:
     # visudo -c

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. COMMAND CHEAT SHEET
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Action                         Command
==============================   ===============================================
  Edit main sudoers              visudo
  Edit drop-in file              visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/<filename>
  Validate syntax                visudo -c
  List current privileges        sudo -l
  Find executable path           which <command>  OR  command -v <command>
  Switch to root shell           sudo -i
  Inspect authentication logs    grep sudo /var/log/secure
  Query systemd journal          journalctl _COMM=sudo

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. AUDITING & LOGGING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sudo logs all execution attempts and privilege escalations.

Log Locations on AlmaLinux / RHEL:
  - Security Log:      /var/log/secure
  - Systemd Journal:   journalctl _COMM=sudo

Example Query:
  # grep sudo /var/log/secure

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. SUMMARY OF BEST PRACTICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. ALWAYS edit with visudo and validate with 'visudo -c'.
2. Enforce LEAST PRIVILEGE: grant specific commands, not full root access.
3. Use FULL PATHS for all executables (e.g., /usr/bin/systemctl).
4. Utilize /etc/sudoers.d/ drop-in files for custom rules.
5. Prefer GROUP-BASED administration for enterprise scalability.
6. Restrict NOPASSWD to automated non-interactive tasks.
7. Regularly audit user access with 'sudo -l' and monitor security log files.

